// Multi-output gather — TWO loop-output arrays on ONE worker->host
// channel. The dedicated fixture for the TASK-0389.01 FIFO ordering
// residual.
//
// What this example demonstrates
// ------------------------------
//   - A single compute worker `w0` produces TWO distinct arrays `p`
//     and `q` INSIDE the same `for i` loop, both consumed by `host`
//     AFTER the loop (the `save_pq` effectful kernel). Under a
//     distributed schedule that places the loop on `w0` and the I/O on
//     `host`, BOTH `p` and `q` cross the SAME (w0 -> host) channel as
//     LOOP OUTPUTS: their host-side Push is hoisted out PAST the `for i`
//     Repeat (the `splice_pushes_global` "cut" branch).
//   - This is the precise shape the TASK-0389 root fix left
//     FIFO-incorrect (TASK-0389.01 fixed it): >=2 data on ONE channel,
//     both cut-hoisted past the SAME Repeat. `p` is produced (and
//     pushed) before `q`, so
//     producer-rank order is [p, q]. Before TASK-0389.01 the naive
//     "insert the Push immediately after the Repeat" splice REVERSED
//     co-hoisted Pushes — the host sent {q, p} while the worker waited
//     {p, q} (the rank-sorted order from `build_waits_for_op`). On a
//     strict-FIFO backend (mp-tcp-bufsync / mp-tcp-poll via
//     `wire::read_msg_expect`) that is a fail-LOUD seq/tag mismatch
//     panic; on the per-seq-demux event backends it would be masked.
//   - The TASK-0389.01 fix makes `splice_after_repeat` APPEND each new
//     Push after any already-spliced Pushes at the same insertion point
//     and feeds the Pushes in producer-rank order, so the host's
//     textual (= wire-send) Push order EQUALS producer-rank order ==
//     the worker's Wait order. FIFO-correct by construction for ANY
//     loop-output nesting.
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate
// --------------------------------------
//   - Iteration-space partitioning (no `partition=`). A single `w0`
//     runs the whole loop; the point is two outputs on ONE channel, not
//     many workers. Multi-worker gather is example 03 / 17.
//   - Data-dependent reads (that is example 17-spmv).
//
// Numeric choice: i32 with wrapping arithmetic (PRD §10.1 — integer
// determinism). `p[i] = a[i] + a[i]`, `q[i] = a[i] + a[i] + a[i]`
// (doubling and tripling expressed via the same scalar `add` kernel so
// the example stays single-kernel-simple and obviously correct).

const N : usize = 64;      // array length; 64 * 4 bytes per array.

// Whole-program input/output arrays. Single-assignment per PRD §6.2.1:
// each of `p[i]`, `q[i]` is assigned exactly once.
data a : i32[N];           // input
data p : i32[N];           // loop output 1 (produced FIRST -> lower rank)
data q : i32[N];           // loop output 2 (produced SECOND -> higher rank)


// Scalar add — pure, reorderable. Reused for both the doubling (p) and
// the partial that feeds the tripling (q).
kernel add : (i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// I/O kernels — effectful (ordering preserved, never duplicated).
// `save_pq` takes BOTH output arrays so the single host consumer reads
// both off the one (w0 -> host) channel; it writes `p` to the first N
// words of output.bin and `q` to the next N words.
kernel load_a  : ()                   -> i32[N] effectful;
kernel save_pq : (i32[N], i32[N])     -> ()     effectful;


// Dataflow.
a <-- load_a();

// Two loop outputs, p BEFORE q (declaration/statement order fixes the
// producer rank: rank(p) < rank(q)).
for i : 0 .. N {
    p[i] <-- add(a[i], a[i]);       // p = 2a
    q[i] <-- add(p[i], a[i]);       // q = p + a = 3a
}

// Single host consumer of BOTH arrays — one (w0 -> host) channel.
save_pq(p, q);
